---
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "Why so little | Project Canon";
const pageDescription =
  "Why durable memory for coding agents should be a handful of notes: most knowledge already has a better home, copies of the code rot, decision records bind the owner, and a record read up front costs every session.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <SiteHeader root="/" />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="essay">
      <span class="mono-label">Why so little</span>
      <h1>A memory that owns almost nothing.</h1>
      <p class="essay-sub">
        The argument, and the field evidence behind it: why the durable
        record must hold only what no other home can — and why that is what
        keeps it true.
      </p>

      <h2>Sessions are amnesiac</h2>
      <p class="opener">
        A coding agent's working memory is its context window, and the
        context window dies with the session. Whatever the agent understood
        about your system — why the gateway exists, why two services share a
        key, why a cache was ripped out — is discarded at the end of the run.
        The next session starts from the repository, and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repository holds more than it seems. Code says what the system
        does; tests say what must not regress; a comment says why one line
        is odd; a commit body says why a change was made and what was
        rejected; a runbook beside the tooling says how to recover. What is
        left over is small: a guarantee several owners rely on that no single
        file can state, a gap the owner accepted on purpose, an approach
        measured and dropped, a plan approved but not yet built.
      </p>

      <h2>Every kind of knowledge has a first home</h2>
      <p>
        Canon's rules are mostly a routing table for knowledge. How to work
        here goes in the instruction file, in the owner's words. Behavior,
        limits and error cases go in code and a test named for the behavior.
        Why a change was made goes in its commit. Only the leftovers become a
        Canon note, and session notes go to an ignored scratch directory that
        nobody cites. Each home is the one a later reader will actually meet:
        the test fails, the comment sits beside the code, <code>git log -S</code>
        finds the reason.
      </p>

      <h2>Copies rot, and so do records</h2>
      <p>
        Documentation as inventory fails twice: first the copy drifts from
        the code, then readers learn the copy lies and stop consulting it,
        including the parts that were true. An earlier Canon tried to fix
        that with process — a manifest every session read first, front
        matter stamped with the commit it was verified against, immutable
        decision records, per-feature specs holding every limit. One adopter
        followed it for 30 days. Canon was touched in 488 of 639 commits;
        about half of all canon file touches were stamp bumps; agents read
        about 23k tokens before any code; 271 decision records piled up; the
        specs drifted to wrong production defaults anyway.
      </p>
      <p>
        The records did worse than cost. An approval record said any other
        path voided it, and an agent reverted a finished retirement 18
        minutes after it landed. A rule an agent had written for itself bound
        the next agent. Immutability made the oldest text the strongest.
      </p>

      <h2>The owner outranks the record</h2>
      <p>
        So authority is now plain. For what should be true: the owner's
        current instruction, then the rules, then the notes. For what is true
        now: code and tests. A note that disagrees with the owner is
        mentioned once and then fixed or deleted in the same change; it can
        never grant permission, veto the owner or be cited to delay them.
        Only the owner's words make a rule, and decisions get no records at
        all: the reason and the rejected alternative go in the commit body,
        and at most one clause goes in the document the decision constrains.
        A reversal edits that clause in place. Git is the history.
      </p>

      <h2>Read nothing up front</h2>
      <p>
        A record does no work from outside the context window, but a record
        read whole spends the very resource the task needs. The rules are the
        only up-front context. A task that touches a cross-module guarantee,
        a production operation or a previously tried approach lists
        <code>canon/</code> and reads the note whose name matches; a task that
        touches none reads nothing. The notes scale; the cost of not needing
        them is zero.
      </p>

      <h2>Small by rule, not by tooling</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing enforced the old rules, and agents followed them anyway — the
        stamps were never checked by any tool, yet thousands were written. So
        the lever is what the rules demand at the moment of writing, not a
        gate afterwards. A change must fix or delete every Canon sentence it
        makes false; moves, renames and refactors oblige nothing. The
        directory is flat, at most twelve notes of about a thousand words
        unless the owner says otherwise, and at the limit a note is added
        only by merging or removing one. There is no checker to satisfy, so
        there are no commits that exist only to satisfy it.
      </p>

      <h2>Drift and silence</h2>
      <p>
        Two failure modes remain. When code contradicts a note, it matters
        what the note was: a stated guarantee is a finding to report and
        resolve as the owner directs; a plain description is merely stale,
        so fix it or delete it. And when the record is silent, silence
        outranks invention: absent policy is a gap to surface, not a blank to
        fill. A guessed limit, once written, becomes load-bearing — authority
        without an author.
      </p>

      <p class="essay-close">
        Canon is small because the truth that has no other home is small.
        Everything else already lives somewhere better — and is allowed to
        churn.
      </p>

      <a class="btn btn-ghost essay-back" href="/">Back to the overview</a>
    </article>

    <SiteFooter />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
